# 3 entries, one genuinely distinct fact per line.
why do we procrastinate | definition | Procrastination is not laziness, and the distinction matters because the two are usually confused and treated with the same useless advice. A lazy person does not want to do the thing. Someone procrastinating wants to do it, would rather not be doing it right now, and cannot start, which is a different problem with a different cause. The most defensible account is that the task is emotionally unpleasant rather than difficult, and starting requires doing the unpleasant part immediately, so a person keeps trading a definite small discomfort now for an uncertain one later. This is why the usual advice about deadlines is not the answer: a deadline increases the unpleasantness and therefore the avoidance. What tends to work is making the first step small enough that the resistance does not engage, and treating a finished bad version as something that can be improved, which changes the question from whether to start into how much further to go. | delay, lazy, start, task, avoid, deadline, emotion | why do we procrastinate, how do i stop procrastinating, am i lazy or procrastinating | palampok | pig-ugma
lying | definition | People lie mostly by omission and by misdirection rather than by saying the opposite of the truth, which is why a person who has not fabricated anything may still be dishonest. The reason lying is common is that it reliably works in the short run: the benefit is immediate and the cost is usually deferred or never collected, and a social context where being pleasant matters more than being accurate pushes people towards the softer option. It is also not always deliberate, because a person who believes something is more often wrong than a person who is lying about it, and self-report is unreliable on this point in both directions. The useful distinction for a listener is between a claim that is mistaken, which can be corrected with information, and one that is constructed, which cannot, and the sign is whether the story changes when new detail arrives. | honest, truth, deceive, omit, misdirect, belief, social | why do people lie, is lying always bad, how to tell if someone is lying | magsaysay | magsaysay
